--TEST--
`echo` replaces every control byte of a computed escape sequence with a visible glyph, and `Core\Cli::escape` answers the same bytes as the sink it launders for — `rule:tooling/terminal-output-is-a-sink`, `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:tooling/terminal-output-is-a-sink`: standard output is a sink, and a control byte is replaced by
// its U+2400-block Control Picture *before* a byte reaches the stream. The
// rule is uniform — no qualifier gates it and no tty test does either — so a
// plain literal is enough to show it.
//
// The sequence is built from code points rather than written as "\e": § 1's
// second mechanism makes an `ESC` in a string literal a compile error, so a
// computed sequence is the only path left to run time, and it is also the
// attack the ADR's *Context* names — a title report, `ESC ] 0 ; … BEL`, which
// is CVE-2022-46387's shape.
string $esc = Core\Str::fromCodePoint(27);
string $bel = Core\Str::fromCodePoint(7);
string $attack = $esc . "]0;owned" . $bel;

// Every command byte comes out as a glyph and the human-readable text between
// them is untouched: this is not an escaper of visible text.
echo $attack, "\n";

// § 1's named launderer is the same table reached as a value. `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`
// requires a launderer to name one sink; this one names the terminal, and the
// property that would otherwise drift silently is that it answers exactly what
// that sink writes.
string $laundered = Core\Cli::escape($attack);
echo $laundered, "\n";

// Nothing was dropped, so an operator sees that something was there — the
// half a substitution written as a deletion would fail while still printing
// plausibly above. Asked of the laundered value, which is a plain `string`.
echo Core\Str::contains($laundered, $esc) ? "leaked" : "no raw escape", "\n";

// The two rows that pass through, which is what keeps the rule from
// reformatting ordinary output.
echo Core\Cli::escape("first\nsecond"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
␛]0;owned␇
␛]0;owned␇
no raw escape
first
second
